L337: University of Auckland, Auckland, New Zealand
L338: King Abdulaziz Medical City, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia
L339: OBJECTIVE:
L340: Vitamin C has been linked to alterations in platelet count and aggregation behavior. Given recent findings suggesting an association between vitamin C and adverse outcomes in patients with septic shock, we aimed to investigate whether vitamin C influences mortality in septic patients through its impact on platelets.
L341: DESIGN:
L342: Post hoc analysis of the Lessening Organ Dysfunction With Vitamin C (LOVIT) randomized trial (clinicaltrials.gov NCT03680274).
L343: SETTING:
L344: Multicenter international study.
L345: PATIENTS:
L346: Patients were included with an ICU stay of more than 24 hours, confirmed or suspected infection, vasopressor requirement, and availability of platelet count data.
L347: INTERVENTION:
L348: Vitamin C (50 mg/kg body weight) every 6 hours for 4 days, or placebo.
L349: MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS:
L350: Of the 863 patients enrolled in the LOVIT trial, 859 had available platelet count data at any time. Although the longitudinal trajectory of platelet count was significantly associated with 28-day mortality (hazard ratio 0.97 per 10 × 10 9 /L increase, 95% CI, 0.96–0.98), there was no interaction between the effect of vitamin C on mortality and either platelet count at baseline or over time.
L351: CONCLUSIONS:
L352: These results do not support the hypothesis that vitamin C administration increases mortality risk by affecting platelet count.
L353: Research Report
L354: blood coagulation disorder; blood platelets; mortality; organ failure; sepsis; shock
L355: KEY POINTS
L356: Question:  Does vitamin C influence mortality by affecting platelet count in patients with sepsis?
L357: Findings:  This secondary analysis of the Lessening Organ Dysfunction With Vitamin C trial found that while a lower platelet count over time was associated with an increased risk of mortality, there was no evidence that vitamin C alters platelet count or affects mortality through changes in platelet count.
L358: Meaning:  Vitamin C does not influence mortality through modulation of platelet count.
L359: MATERIAL AND METHODS
L360: Study Design and Settings
L361: This study was a post hoc analysis of the randomized, placebo-controlled LOVIT trial (clinicaltrials.gov NCT03680274), conducted across 35 medical and surgical ICUs in Canada, France, and New Zealand. The LOVIT trial was conducted in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki and was first approved by the Comité d’éthique de la recherche du center intégré universitaire de santé et de services sociaux de l’Estrie—center hospitalier universitaire de Sherbrooke (reference MP-31-2019-2945) on September 25, 2018, and it was subsequently approved by the ethics committee at each center. Participation required informed consent obtained from the patient or their legal representatives, or through initial deferred consent.
L362: Patients
L363: The LOVIT trial included adult patients (18 yr old or older) with suspected or confirmed infection as the primary diagnosis leading to ICU admission, requiring a vasopressor infusion, and having an ICU stay of more than 24 hours. For this post hoc analysis, only individuals with available platelet count measurements were included.
L364: Intervention and Outcome
L365: Patients were randomized 1:1 into either a vitamin C treatment group or a placebo control group. Individuals in the treatment group received 50 mg/kg of body weight of vitamin C IV every 6 hours until ICU discharge or for a maximum of 4 days. Those in the control group received a matching placebo infusion. Group randomization and platelet count measured on the day of enrollment (day 1), and on days 2, 3, 4, 7, 10, 14, and 28 served as exposures. The primary endpoint for this analysis was 28-day mortality.
L366: Statistical Analysis
L367: Kruskal-Wallis and chi-square tests were used, as appropriate, to compare characteristics between patients with a platelet count less than or equal to 170 × 10⁹/L and those with greater than 170 × 10⁹/L at the time of enrollment. Cox regression models, including an interaction between treatment group and platelet count (as a continuous variable), were used to investigate the relationship between treatment effect and platelet count and their association with 28-day mortality. In the second step, a linear mixed-effects model was used to model longitudinal platelet trajectories and their interaction with treatment group. This approach was integrated with a Cox proportional hazards model within a Bayesian joint modeling framework, incorporating longitudinal platelet count trajectories and treatment allocation as independent variables, with 28-day mortality as the endpoint ( 5 ). Statistical analyses were conducted by a statistician (R.P.) using SAS (Version 9.4; SAS Institute, Cary, NC) and the JMbayes2 package in R (Version 4.3.1; R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria).
L368: RESULTS
L369: Of the 863 patients enrolled in the trial, platelet count measurements at any time during follow-up were available for 859 patients, whereas day 1 measurements were available for 782 patients. Median platelet count at day of enrollment was 170 [interquartile range (IQR) 106–252] × 10 9 /L. In patients with baseline platelets greater than 170 × 10 9 /L, 31% (58/188) of those in the vitamin C group and 21% (42/202) of those in the placebo group died. Among patients with platelet counts less than or equal to 170 × 10⁹/L, mortality was 44% (88/198) in the vitamin C group and 43% (84/194) in the placebo group. Patient characteristics at baseline were comparable between individuals receiving vitamin C and those receiving placebo, as reported elsewhere ( 1 ).  Table  1  presents characteristics and outcomes stratified by platelet count at baseline (> 170 or ≤ 170 × 10⁹/L).  Table S1  ( https://links.lww.com/CCX/B546 ) shows patient characteristics across the different treatment allocations.
L370: TABLE 1. Patient Characteristics at Baseline and Outcome
L371: The Cox regression model revealed a statistically significant association of day 1 platelet count with mortality (hazard ratio [HR] 0.98 per 10 × 10 9 /L increase, 95% CI, 0.97–0.99), but the association of vitamin C exposure with mortality was not statistically significant (HR 1.26; 95% CI, 0.99–1.60), with no interaction between these exposures ( p  = 0.87).
L372: The Bayesian joint model, which included all patients with available platelet count measurements during the study period ( n  = 859), indicated no interaction between platelet count and the treatment group. After removing the interaction term, the longitudinal trajectory of platelet count over time was strongly associated with mortality (HR 0.97 per 10 × 10 9 /L increase; 95% credible interval [CrI], 0.96–0.98), whereas no association was observed with allocation to vitamin C (HR 1.18; 95% CrI, 0.87–1.61).  Figure  1  illustrates platelet count trajectories over time in the randomized groups, stratified by survival status.
L373: Figure 1. Distribution of platelets over time stratified by 28-day mortality. Platelet counts over time presented as mean ±  se  of the mean for patients allocated to the treatment (vitamin C) and control (placebo) groups, stratified by 28-day mortality status (deceased vs. alive). Although the Bayesian joint model demonstrated an association between longitudinal platelet trajectory and mortality, there was no interaction between platelet count and treatment allocation.
L374: DISCUSSION
L375: This post hoc analysis of the LOVIT trial confirms an association between low baseline platelet count and falling platelet count over time and increased 28-day mortality, but does not identify a significant interaction between either baseline or temporal changes in platelet count and the effect of vitamin C administration.
L376: Vitamin C has been recognized for its role as a scavenger of reactive oxygen species, providing a strong physiological rationale for its potential organ-protective effects in patients with sepsis. However, meta-analyses have not found high-certainty evidence supporting a mortality reduction with vitamin C ( 6 ), and guidelines have conditionally recommended against its use in sepsis ( 7 ). This controversy has been amplified by the findings of the LOVIT trial, which reported an adverse effect of vitamin C on a composite endpoint of persistent organ dysfunction or mortality at 28 days in a moderately large patient cohort ( 1 ). To date, no pathophysiological explanation has been determined for these findings. This post hoc analysis was designed to explore the potential mechanism of lower platelet count to explain the harm attributed to vitamin C.
L377: Although vitamin C has been shown in rodent studies to exert protective effects on organ dysfunction in the heart, lungs, kidneys, and brain, its impact on the coagulation system remains controversial. Vitamin C has been reported to both enhance platelet aggregation and exhibit anti-aggregatory effects, in a dose-dependent manner ( 4 ,  8 ). Given that disrupted coagulation is a central feature of sepsis pathophysiology, an additional impact on platelets attributed to vitamin C could potentially worsen coagulation dysfunction in this patient cohort, providing a plausible explanation for the unfavorable outcomes observed in the LOVIT trial. To test this hypothesis, we selected platelet count as a surrogate marker for coagulation dysfunction, as it is a well-established prognostic marker in sepsis ( 9 ).
L378: Our analysis demonstrates that low platelet counts at baseline and over time are associated with mortality in patients with sepsis, but platelet count over time is not significantly altered by exposure to vitamin C. This finding contributes to the ongoing effort to clarify the pathophysiological mechanisms by which vitamin C may have caused harm in the LOVIT trial and to identify patient subgroups that may be particularly likely to be harmed by (or to benefit from) this treatment ( 10 ). Given the persistent uncertainty surrounding the evidence for vitamin C use in patients with sepsis—and in light of expert recommendations for further clinical trials—identifying factors associated with adverse events is essential for incorporating prognostic enrichment in any future studies ( 2 ,  3 ).
L379: Although platelet count is commonly used as a marker of coagulopathy in sepsis, the generalizability of our findings to the coagulation cascade is limited by the absence of functional assessments, such as platelet function testing, fibrinogen levels,  d- dimer, and thromboelastography. In addition, this investigation focused exclusively on mortality as the primary outcome, leaving the potential effect of vitamin C on the persistent organ dysfunction component of the composite endpoint used in the LOVIT trial unexamined. Further investigations, including functional assessments of the coagulation system and outcomes directly related to impaired coagulation, are necessary to better understand any potential adverse interactions between vitamin C and the coagulation cascade.
L380: CONCLUSIONS
L381: In summary, this analysis confirms the association between thrombocytopenia and mortality in patients with sepsis but does not provide evidence that vitamin C affects platelet count or that platelet count mediates an increased mortality in patients receiving vitamin C.
